 number graph kneser\n\n### Abstract\n\nFor $k \\geq 1$ and $n \\geq 2k$, the well known Kneser graph $\\operatorname{KG}(n,k)$ has all $k$-element subsets of an $n$-element set as vertices; two such subsets are adjacent if they are disjoint. Schrijver constructed a vertex-critical subgraph $\\operatorname{SG}(n,k)$ of $\\operatorname{KG}(n,k)$ with the same chromatic number. In this paper, we compute the diameter of the graph $\\operatorname{SG}(2k+r,k)$ with $r \\geq 1$. We obtain an exact value of the diameter of $\\operatorname{SG}(2k+r,k)$ when $r \\in \\{1,2\\}$ or when $r \\geq k-3$. For the remained cases, when $3 \\leq r \\leq k-4$, we obtain that the diameter of $\\operatorname{SG}(2k+r,k)$ belongs to the integer interval $[4..k-r-1]$.\n\nMaintained by Per Alexandersson, per@symmetricfunctions.com--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOn the diameter of Schrijver graphs (https://arxiv.org/abs/2112.01884)\nciteturn5academia13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4.9 years ago; Title: On the diameter of Schrijver graphs ... In this paper, we compute the diameter of the graph $\\operatorname{SG}(2k+r,k)$ with $r \\geq 1$.\nTitle: On the diameter of Schrijver graphs\nAuthors: Agustina Victoria Ledezma, Adrián Pastine, Pablo Torres, Mario Valencia-Pabon\nDate: Fri Dec  3 12:48:33 2021\n\nFor $k \\geq 1$ and $n \\geq 2k$, the well known Kneser graph $\\operatorname{KG}(n,k)$ has all $k$-element subsets of an $n$-element set as vertices; two such subsets are adjacent if they are disjoint. Schrijver constructed a vertex-critical subgraph $\\operatorname{SG}(n,k)$ of $\\operatorname{KG}(n,k)$ with the same chromatic number. In this paper, we compute the diameter of the graph $\\operatorname{SG}(2k+r,k)$ with $r \\geq 1$. We obtain an exact value of the diameter of $\\operatorname{SG}(2k+r,k)$ when $r \\in \\{1,2\\}$ or when $r \\geq k-3$. For the remained cases, when $3 \\leq r \\leq k-4$, we obtain that the diameter of $\\operato